ByteOrder.nativeOrder() on the wasm-gc target, without JavaScript.

TeaVM asks JavaScript for it there: a short written through an Int16Array
over an ArrayBuffer and read back as bytes.  A host other than JavaScript
has no ArrayBuffer (the WASI adapter stops the program at it), and the answer
is not the host's to give: a direct buffer is in the module's linear memory,
which wasm defines as little-endian.  ByteBuffer.order() asks for it.

test/teavm/fixes/byteorder.

--- a/teavm-classlib/org/teavm/classlib/java/nio/TByteOrder.java
+++ b/teavm-classlib/org/teavm/classlib/java/nio/TByteOrder.java
@@ -33,7 +33,11 @@ public final class TByteOrder {
 
     public static TByteOrder nativeOrder() {
         if (nativeOrder == null) {
-            if (PlatformDetector.isJavaScript() || PlatformDetector.isWebAssemblyGC()) {
+            if (PlatformDetector.isWebAssemblyGC()) {
+                // a direct buffer is in linear memory, which is little-endian, and
+                // a host need not be JavaScript to run the module
+                nativeOrder = LITTLE_ENDIAN;
+            } else if (PlatformDetector.isJavaScript()) {
                 var buffer = new ArrayBuffer(2);
                 var shortArray = new Int16Array(buffer);
                 shortArray.set(0, (short) 1);
